|~ What is declared under `::number`: the refusal of its
   constructor, and the verbs of numbers, each serving a number. The head
   of a verb is its declaration, which the runtime executes before the
   primitive runs; the refusals of a verb sit beside it, the head raising
   the one its site declares at the place it checks, and the primitive
   the rest. ~|

:number/PayloadNotNumberError
  |~~ `::number` reads its payload as the number it is; a payload of
      another kind is refused, and text is read by `parseJson`.
      ~(
        ::number"5"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::number/PayloadNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:number/add
  |~~ Adds two numbers: `a | add b` computes a + b, and the full form
      `add a b` reads both modifiers against the value in the pipe.

      ~(10 | add 3 | eq 13)
      ~({:x 10 :y 3} | add /x /y | eq 13)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:addend ::number | :returns ::number | ::builtin{})

:number/AddLeftNotNumberError
  |~~ `add` adds numbers: a vector adds element by element, `* add 1`,
      and sums by `sum`; a string is joined by `append`.

      ~(
        "hello" | add 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::number/AddLeftNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:number/AddRightNotNumberError
  |~~ The addend of `add` is a number; two fields of one map add by
      `add /price /tax`, both read from it.

      ~(
        1 | add "x"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::number/AddRightNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:number/AddResultNotFiniteError
  |~~ `add` answered outside the finite doubles a number of qlang
      is: both operands are numbers, held as `:leftValue` and
      `:rightValue`, and the size of the answer is what the site
      refuses.

      ~(
        1e308 | add 1e308
        !| [type /leftValue]
        |  eq [::number/AddResultNotFiniteError 1e308]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:number/sub
  |~~ Subtracts the subtrahend from the subject, the minuend.

      ~(10 | sub 3 | eq 7)
      ~({:x 10 :y 3} | sub /x /y | eq 7)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:subtrahend ::number | :returns ::number | ::builtin{})

:number/SubLeftNotNumberError
  |~~ `sub` subtracts from a number; a vector subtracts element by
      element, `* sub 1`.

      ~(
        "hello" | sub 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::number/SubLeftNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:number/SubRightNotNumberError
  |~~ The subtrahend of `sub` is a number; two fields of one map
      subtract by `sub /total /paid`.

      ~(
        1 | sub "x"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::number/SubRightNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:number/SubResultNotFiniteError
  |~~ `sub` answered outside the finite doubles a number of qlang
      is: both operands are numbers, held as `:leftValue` and
      `:rightValue`, and the size of the answer is what the site
      refuses.

      ~(
        -1e308 | sub 1e308
        !| [type /leftValue]
        |  eq [::number/SubResultNotFiniteError -1e308]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:number/mul
  |~~ Multiplies two numbers.

      ~(10 | mul 3 | eq 30)
      ~({:x 5 :y 4} | mul /x /y | eq 20)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:multiplier ::number | :returns ::number | ::builtin{})

:number/MulLeftNotNumberError
  |~~ `mul` multiplies numbers; a vector multiplies element by element,
      `* mul 2`.

      ~(
        "hello" | mul 2
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::number/MulLeftNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:number/MulRightNotNumberError
  |~~ The multiplier of `mul` is a number; two fields of one map
      multiply by `mul /price /qty`.

      ~(
        1 | mul "x"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::number/MulRightNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:number/MulResultNotFiniteError
  |~~ `mul` answered outside the finite doubles a number of qlang
      is: both operands are numbers, held as `:leftValue` and
      `:rightValue`, and the size of the answer is what the site
      refuses.

      ~(
        1e308 | mul 10
        !| [type /leftValue]
        |  eq [::number/MulResultNotFiniteError 1e308]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:number/div
  |~~ Divides the subject, the dividend, by the divisor, and refuses a
      divisor of zero with `::number/DivisionByZeroError`.

      ~(10 | div 2 | eq 5)
      ~({:x 20 :y 4} | div /x /y | eq 5)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:divisor ::number | :returns ::number | ::builtin{})

:number/DivLeftNotNumberError
  |~~ `div` divides a number; a vector divides element by element,
      `* div 2`.

      ~(
        "hello" | div 2
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::number/DivLeftNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:number/DivRightNotNumberError
  |~~ The divisor of `div` is a number; two fields of one map divide by
      `div /sum /count`.

      ~(
        1 | div "x"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::number/DivRightNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:number/DivResultNotFiniteError
  |~~ `div` answered outside the finite doubles a number of qlang
      is: both operands are numbers, held as `:leftValue` and
      `:rightValue`, and the size of the answer is what the site
      refuses.

      ~(
        1e308 | div 0.0001
        !| [type /leftValue]
        |  eq [::number/DivResultNotFiniteError 1e308]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:number/DivisionByZeroError
  |~~ A division by zero has no finite answer; a divisor that may be
      zero is tested first, `if (/count | eq 0) ~(0) ~(div /sum /count)`.

      ~(
        10 | div 0
        !| [type]
        |  eq [::number/DivisionByZeroError]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:number/round
  |~~ The number rounded half away from zero to the digits after the
      point the call gives, none when it gives none; a negative count
      rounds to tens and beyond.

      ~(3.14159 | round 2 | eq 3.14)
      ~(2.5 | round | eq 3)
      ~(-2.5 | round | eq -3)
      ~(1234 | round -2 | eq 1200)
      ~(-0.4 | round | print | eq "0")
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:digits 0 | :returns ::number | ::builtin{})

:number/RoundSubjectNotNumberError
  |~~ `round` rounds a number; a string holding one is read first by
      `parseJson`.
      ~(
        "2.5" | round
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::number/RoundSubjectNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:number/RoundDigitsNotIntegerError
  |~~ The digits of `round` are a whole number, `round 2`.
      ~(
        2.5 | round 0.5
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::number/RoundDigitsNotIntegerError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:number/gt
  |~~ Whether the subject is greater than the number the call gives.

      ~(10 | gt 5 | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::number | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{})

:number/gte
  |~~ Whether the subject is greater than or equal to the number the
      call gives.

      ~(10 | gte 10 | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::number | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{})

:number/lt
  |~~ Whether the subject is less than the number the call gives.

      ~(5 | lt 10 | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::number | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{})

:number/lte
  |~~ Whether the subject is less than or equal to the number the call
      gives.

      ~(10 | lte 10 | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::number | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{})
